  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Gulfstream Aerospace has $313M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Gulfstream Aerospace received 79 awards in the same period, with an average award size of $4M, and obligations are up 134.8% year over year. The current-year totals are partial because fiscal year to date figures are reported. Gulfstream Aerospace has concentrated obligations from the Department of Homeland Security and the Department of Defense, which together account for the majority of FY2026 to date spending. Gulfstream Aerospace records activity across transportation-related NAICS codes, led by Commercial Air, Rail, and Water Transportation Equipment Rental and Leasing and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation, alongside smaller amounts in Aircraft Manufacturing and several publishing and training categories. The tables report award values for the largest awards and expiring contracts as total contract values rather than as current-year obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Gulfstream Aerospace's biggest federal customers?",
        "body": "Department of Homeland Security is the top buyer, obligating $185M in FY2026 to date. Department of Defense is the second-largest buyer, obligating $126M. Department of Justice, National Aeronautics and Space Administration, and Department of Transportation appear with much smaller amounts."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What product and service categories does Gulfstream Aerospace sell to the government?",
        "body": "Commercial Air, Rail, and Water Transportation Equipment Rental and Leasing is the largest market by obligations at $188M. Other Support Activities for Air Transportation follows at $101M, with Aircraft Manufacturing at $24.8M and smaller amounts in Software Publishers, All Other Publishers, and Professional and Management Development Training."
      }
    ]
  },
